WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:12.650
Hi, everyone. Welcome to the closing webinar of
the "Imaging Science" series. So, we're

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.790
about to get started.
We have lots of cool images from

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:21.529
researchers to show you and the ones
that you all have sent in, as well. So,

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:25.939
just scroll through a selection of those.
With that, I'm going to hand this

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:33.260
over to Erika Reinfeld, who's going
to start us off.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.800
[Erika] Thank you so much, Brian. All right.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:41.180
We are so excited to be here
with you. So, two weeks ago, we hosted the

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.320
opening webinar. I am actually not
looking at the poll, so I don't know how

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.760
many of you attended it. If you didn't, I
hope you'll log on to the museum site

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.010
and check it out.
We hosted that with two fabulous

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.930
photographers, Keith Ellenbogen and
Felice Frankel, and we are really excited

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:07.130
to close this week out with the research
counterpart to that. So, our researchers,

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.430
our guests researchers, will be sharing a
couple of their own science photographs

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:15.620
with you. Images that came out of active
research here at MIT. And then they'll be

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:19.010
talking to you about some of the images
that they saw on this two-week

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.660
exploration that they found really
intriguing. Before we jump into their

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.320
presentations, I did want to call out a
couple of the themes from the

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:33.800
opening webinar by contact for context
for this conversation. So, one of the

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.970
central ideas that we talked about was
the idea of photography and imaging as a

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:42.590
tool of science; helping us to make
observations and to communicate

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:47.150
scientific ideas. As science
photographers, both Keith and Felice

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:51.110
notice science everywhere. But, I thought
it was really interesting that Keith

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:56.330
identifies as an artist, Felice does not.
So, they both talked about the

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:03.229
intentionality of a "good" science
photograph and that an important

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:08.899
element of that is being intentional
about your photography, but also leaving

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:12.650
yourself open to discovery. I think
that's very characteristic of a lot of

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:17.300
the images we're going to be looking at
today. One of the comments that came

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:22.760
through in the chat that I really liked,
was that many of the photographs that we

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.780
saw were, in some ways, unintentional art;
they weren't designed to be art, but they

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:32.000
still had an artistic component to it. It
was a combination of the

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.500
expected and the unexpected that come
together to tell the story. We also

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.370
talked a little bit about maintaining
the scientific integrity of a photograph...

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:44.720
of the scientific subject... when taking a
photograph, but then still using the

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:51.590
photograph to tell a story. Then
finally, we talked a little bit about the

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.459
value of bringing both people and ideas
together to create a photograph and make

00:02:55.459 --> 00:03:00.079
sense of what's being represented in the
world. So, [clap] in that spirit, we're going to

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:05.209
bring in our guests. As they present
their images, you are welcome to use the

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:10.220
Q&amp;A feature, down at the bottom of your
screen, to address questions, either to an

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:14.090
individual or to the whole group, and
we'll try to get to, at least a handful

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:18.110
of those, after everyone has presented
their image. So, we're going to start with

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:23.780
Sudha Kumari, who is a researcher at the
Koch Institute, where I work. You can see

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.739
the public galleries behind me. This is
our annual Image Awards exhibition of

00:03:27.739 --> 00:03:33.109
biomechanical images. I actually use
Sudha's winning image from the Image

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:38.480
Awards often to represent the
Image Awards competition as a whole and

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.549
just as an example of a scientific
photograph. So, I'll stop talking and let

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:47.530
Sudha tell you and show you more.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:56.220
[Sudha] Hi, everyone. So, I am Sudha Kumari. I hope
you are able to hear me and hope you're

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.840
having a wonderful weekend. So, I am a
research scientist at the Irvine Lab

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.030
here at the Koch Institute of
Integrative Cancer Research. In the

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:10.200
Irvine Lab, we believe that our immune
system is a powerful agent to fight

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.650
diseases -- different types of diseases
like cancer and autoimmune diseases. And

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:20.010
we also believe that when cancer
develops, there's a compromised function

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.730
of immune systems... somehow immune system
is suppressed and that's how cancer

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:29.850
progresses. If we can actually clip
the immune cells, or the cells' immune

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.710
system, to fight back at the cancer,
we can actually tackle cancer eventually.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:38.970
So, what I'm going to share with you
today is actually image, which is

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:43.980
actually a summarized story of my
project that shows many different

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:49.140
subplots in a single image. As to which
immune cells I'm using and how they're

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.560
interacting with the immune cells, or
with the cancer cells, and how what we

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:59.670
hope to achieve using this interaction.
So, I'll just briefly, in a brief moment, I

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:03.800
will share my image with you guys.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:12.349
Yeah. So, basically, my project started
with trying to see how the immune cells,

00:05:12.349 --> 00:05:16.840
(specifically the t-cells, which might
threaten immune cells because they can

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:22.250
directly recognize and kill cancer cells)
how they interact, how they communicate

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:27.740
how they talk to the cancer cells, and
are they able to actually kill cancer

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:32.690
cells? So, I took cancer cells, which are
large cells shown here -- these are cancer

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:38.090
cells here -- in a dish and I threw some
t-cells that would actually recognize

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:42.710
and kill the tumor cells and then let
them interact. It was a very

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.199
interesting interaction to watch because
I could see the t-cells were

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:50.810
actually attacking the tumor cells. I
had already loaded the t-cells with

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:55.340
nanoparticles that have drug that would
increase t-cell function.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:01.099
They'll increase the capability to fight
these tumor cells. I could see that

00:06:01.099 --> 00:06:05.569
the drug-loaded t-cells were beautifully
binding to the tumor cells. So, they were

00:06:05.569 --> 00:06:11.270
recognizing them and they were able to
actually kill tumor cells. So, in the

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:15.979
image, the two different tumor cells -- the
one that is healthy, and the other one,

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:20.530
which is sort of retracting, which is a
sign of it being killed by the t-cells.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:26.840
So, in this image, you could actually see
different part of the processes that

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:31.310
entail my project, which is looking at
the interaction of immune cells, such as

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:37.430
t-cells, or the tumor cells, as well as
seeing how drug-loaded nanoparticles

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:43.759
could help t-cells in their ability to
kill tumor cells. The idea was that

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:47.780
if you can actually see this process in
a dish, hopefully we'll be able to

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:54.020
recapitulate it in the body sometime, in
the models of cancer, and be able to take

00:06:54.020 --> 00:07:00.530
care of disease progression in the real
situation. So, with that, I'd like to thank

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:08.690
you for your attention and give
it back to Erika. Thank you.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:13.159
[Erika] Thank you so much, Sudha. One of the
things I love about this image is that

00:07:13.159 --> 00:07:18.289
it tells a very dramatic science story.
It's a very "good versus evil" battle.

00:07:18.289 --> 00:07:23.300
But it also, is an image that really
comes out of the scientific process. Did

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.629
this experiment that we were doing
actually work?

00:07:26.629 --> 00:07:31.310
So, our next image that we're going to
look at, is also an image of documenting

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:35.449
a scientific process and it's really
looking at the experiment a little more.

00:07:35.449 --> 00:07:41.180
So, I think Matt, if you would like to get
your image all queued up, I'll turn it

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:44.289
over to Matt Victor.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:56.770
[Matt] Thanks, Erika. Just give me one second to
share my image.

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:06.650
So, my name is Matt Victor.
I am an HHMI Hanna Gray Fellow at the

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:11.740
Picower Institute for Learning and
Memory. I have a PhD in neuroscience,

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:17.020
but a lot of the questions I'm interested
in have to do with the developing brain.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:24.380
The approach I take is that I model
neuronal development, or brain

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:33.409
development, with stem cells. The field of
stem cell has really exploded in the

00:08:33.409 --> 00:08:40.669
past decade when the discovery was made
that a fully differentiated adult cell,

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:47.740
for example a skin cell, could be
reprogrammed to become a stem cell and

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:54.380
that stem cell can then be made into all
known cell types in our body. That's

00:08:54.380 --> 00:09:01.190
what we call induced pluripotent stem
cells or iPSCs. So, I work with iPSCs

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:07.029
and I differentiate the stem cells to
become neurons, which are brain cells.

00:09:07.029 --> 00:09:13.850
Neurons have this amazing capacity to
generate electrical signaling and that's

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:20.390
how information is processed in our
brain. So, what I'm showing you here, is an

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:29.120
image of nearly 20,000 neurons. The cell
bodies are kind of white circles, but

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:35.110
these cells, they have a very unique
morphology where they protrude

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:42.260
extensions to make connections between
different cells. So, you can see in here,

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:47.930
it's a droplet of 20,000 cells that have
been allowed to grow and they were

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:54.890
plated on a multi electrode array. So,
these larger black dots that are

00:09:54.890 --> 00:10:01.339
connected wires, are actually reading the
electrical potentials that the cells are

00:10:01.339 --> 00:10:10.670
generating. I'm using the cells as a
proxy for a developing brain cell

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:19.670
to understand how activity shapes their
maturation. So, as we develop, we receive

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:28.160
a lot of input, for example sensory input,
like your mother's touch, and all those

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:32.480
sensory inputs are transformed into
activity that's processed by the brain.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.820
There is reason to think that that's
a very important part of developing a

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:42.680
mature brain. So, we're trying to mimic
that in the dish. So, I think this

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:48.290
image is striking because it conveys a
very difficult technique, which is

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:54.140
generating these cells from stem cells
and getting them to be functional, but it

00:10:54.140 --> 00:11:00.140
also tells you a little bit about the
interconnectivity of the cells -- you

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:04.430
can see this meshes are connected -- and
some of the readouts of my research,

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:08.510
which is to study how electrical
activity changes with maturation in

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:16.560
vitro. So, I'll stop here and chat more later,
if you have more questions.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:22.100
[Erika] Thank you so much, Matt. Yeah. When Matt submitted this to the Image Awards contest, he also

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:25.580
submitted an image that was just the
neurons and it was a beautifully

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:31.550
striking image -- these glowing cell bodies,
and all these interactions, and data that

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:36.500
Matt was talking about. But, the judges
chose this image because they loved how

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:43.100
it documented the process of observing,
as well. And one of the things I talked

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:47.120
about in the opening webinar was making
comparisons across different scales of

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:53.090
science. And so, with that, we're actually
going to move onto another image that

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:58.130
also has a combination of the thing
doing the observing, and the thing

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:04.690
being observed. So, Calvin Leung
is going to show his image.

00:12:05.649 --> 00:12:11.990
[Calvin] Yeah. Thanks, Erika. Hi, everyone. My name
is Calvin. I am a PhD candidate and a

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:15.770
member of the CHIME – FRB
collaboration. So, as you might guess, I'm

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:21.800
an astrophysicist and one of my biggest
science goals is to survey the night sky

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.700
for these mysterious objects called,
"Faster Radio Bursts." These are

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:31.459
brief, bright radio flashes that are
coming from all directions towards Earth

00:12:31.459 --> 00:12:35.240
and we think they come from outside the
Milky Way galaxy. So, they're

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:38.180
fascinating because they're just
milliseconds long, but in that one

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:43.100
millisecond or so that they turn on, they
can sometimes outshine their entire

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:46.730
host galaxy. So, this makes them
extremely powerful, but super mysterious

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:53.500
objects to detect, because they just come
and go and you never see them again. So,

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:59.690
this is a picture of the Canadian
Hydrogen Mapping Intensity Experiment, or

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:06.560
CHIME, and what we do, is we have these four cylinders, which are shaped

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:11.870
like so because they have an enormous
field of view of the sky, unlike your

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:15.830
regular telescope, which just stares in a
tiny, little patch of sky. So, for

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:19.670
reference, your conventional telescope,
that you might see, you know, on the

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:25.370
mountaintops of Mauna Kea or at
Palomar Observatory, might have a field

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:30.980
of view of about a 60th of a degree by a
60th to the degree. But, with our radio

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:37.279
telescope, with its metal
structure, you know, with these

00:13:37.279 --> 00:13:42.140
cylinders, which are each 20 meters
wide and 80 meters long, and the way they

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:48.140
point at the sky, they can actually
survey a two-degree wide strip of the

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:52.970
entire night sky, running north-south. So, we have this massive barcode scanner

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:58.100
on the sky, that surveys different
parts of the sky as the Earth

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:02.720
rotates beneath it. So, this unique
design has allowed us to discover more

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:07.070
fast radio bursts in a year than have
ever been detected in the past decade.

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:11.870
So, this has really transformed the
field and so I wanted to include a

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:16.400
picture of, not just the night sky that
we're so fascinated by, but also the

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:20.740
unique instrument
that lets us perform these

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:25.630
transformative observations. So,
that's sort of the science side of this

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:30.790
picture. On the aesthetic side, one
of the things I really liked about it is

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:35.440
that it conveys a strong sense of
purpose. You see these, like, massive

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:41.740
metal cylinder structures and there's
obviously some purpose to it. Their

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:46.060
irregularity,
you know, gives us a sense of, like,

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:51.100
unity and harmony that I think... that really sends chills down my

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:56.950
spine, even though I think about this
experiment every day. Also, I think, on

00:14:56.950 --> 00:15:02.350
a more personal note, I mean, I really
like this image because it, you know,

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:08.200
because I am, as a scientist, I'm
really interested in instrumentation, and

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:12.779
the development of new instruments,
that can allow us to make

00:15:12.779 --> 00:15:17.700
transformative new science observations,
like the unique design of our

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:24.310
cylindrical telescope. And so, to me,
every time you do astrophysics, it's a

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:30.100
sort of, communion between the telescope
and the night sky. So, I think this is

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:33.640
just a particularly complete way of
expressing that. It's not just the sky,

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:38.380
but it's also what you use to look at it.
And so, you know, in summary, those

00:15:38.380 --> 00:15:46.510
three things -- sort of the science
appeal, the sense of grandeur

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:50.320
that you get, and also that sort of
personal touch make, you know, make me

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:53.180
have this feeling that something 
magical is really going on

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:57.699
here. That's why I showed this photo.
Before I finish, I want to just

00:15:57.699 --> 00:16:02.589
mention that I actually did not take
this. This was taken by one of my very

00:16:02.589 --> 00:16:08.440
close collaborators, Andre Renard, at the
University of Toronto. That's all I've

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:10.899
got.
I'll pass it back to Erika. Thank you.

00:16:10.899 --> 00:16:15.760
[Erika] Thank you so much, Calvin. Yeah. One of the things I think is really interesting -- so it's a

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:20.199
photo of nature, it's a photo of
technology, but somehow, when you put all

00:16:20.199 --> 00:16:25.149
those things together, it's also a very
human photo about our own mission to

00:16:25.149 --> 00:16:30.920
understand the world and to learn more
about the world and create things that

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:37.010
help us appreciate what's going on in
nature. So, with that, I think we're going to

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:42.620
shift gears to some
different engineered cylinders. We're

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:49.370
going to head back to the biological scale
and look at the image of something

00:16:49.370 --> 00:16:54.139
that we built to, not just
understand how the body works, but also

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:59.860
to improve how the body works.
Quinton Smith is our next presenter.

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:05.059
[Quinton] All right. Good afternoon, everyone and thank
you so much for joining today.

00:17:05.059 --> 00:17:09.350
My name is Quinton Smith. I'm a
postdoctoral fellow at MIT's Koch

00:17:09.350 --> 00:17:12.770
Institute and I work under the
mentorship of Sangeeta Bhatia's

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:18.620
laboratory for multi scale regenerative
technologies. So, the particular research

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:23.419
lens that I'm focused in, is liver tissue
engineering. So, the liver is one of

00:17:23.419 --> 00:17:27.319
the coolest organs. It actually has a
unique ability to regenerate. You can

00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:30.950
actually remove two-thirds of the liver
and it will completely regrow.

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:35.510
So, we've actually taken advantage of
that unique property for people who

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:39.950
actually need liver transplants for
survival. So, you can do something called

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:44.090
a split liver transplant, where you can take
a liver from one person and put it in

00:17:44.090 --> 00:17:48.650
another and it can actually grow back on
both of the patients. So, one of the big

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:54.110
difficulties within this field is that
there is over 114 thousand people in the

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:57.260
United States that are in need of this
life-saving surgery. So, there's not

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:01.370
enough donors to support this demand. So,
I'm actually a chemical engineer by

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:04.940
training. So, you kind of think about how
can we create engineering solutions

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:09.190
to really bridge the gap between the
unavailability of these organ systems.

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:13.340
So, in this one here... this image
here, I want to demonstrate one

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:18.080
particular architecture within the liver
as a way to create miniature livers

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:22.790
in the lab that we can actually implant
for patients. So, there's like, kind of a

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:28.040
lot of different intricacies that were
done to really create this picture. So,

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:32.929
what I'm showing here, is an image of
something called a "biliary duct." So, this

00:18:32.929 --> 00:18:36.740
is a very important component within the
liver, that's actually responsible for

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:41.360
carrying bile acid from the liver to the
small intestines, where it actually helps

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:46.100
in, aids in, digestion. So, people who
have liver problems, for example, they

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:49.390
actually get something called jaundice,
where you have yellowing of the eyes, or

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:53.990
yellowing of the skin and nails, and this
is actually that bile acid leaking out.

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:58.549
So, we understand this biliary
architecture is very important. So, what

00:18:58.549 --> 00:19:03.320
I'm showing in this image here, are cells
that make up the bile duct where we can

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:08.210
control their architecture in a very
specific manner. So, this open structure

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:13.220
that we can create is actually on the
order of a human hair size. So, these are

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:18.770
around 150 to 300 microns in diameter.
And how we do it -- we use a pretty cool

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:24.500
technique. We use acupuncture needles and
we cast a biologically relevant material

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:28.640
in which cells thrive in and use the
acupuncture needles as a sacrificial

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:34.640
mold. After we create this mold with the
extracellular matrix that builds up

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:39.409
tissues, we can remove this really small
needle and we're open with perfusable

00:19:39.409 --> 00:19:44.150
systems, where we can seed with these
biliary epithelial cells. So, what I

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:49.040
really like about this image, is that we
can see that, as engineers, that

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:54.440
we can recapitulate the architecture
that we see in biology. So, here's a proof

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:59.210
of concept where we can create these
very two standing structures that mimic

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:03.980
the architecture of the liver itself. So,
I really like this picture and I hope

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:09.020
you guys do and I'm really excited for
the Q&amp;A session afterwards. And, with that,

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:11.500
I'll give it back to Erika. Thank you.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:12.740
[Erika] 
Thank you, Quinton. Yeah. I actually

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:18.169
learned about, like many of the Image
Awards entries, I learned about this

00:20:18.169 --> 00:20:23.059
project, before I saw the image, and I made
Quinton draw me lots of pictures of how

00:20:23.059 --> 00:20:27.679
it all worked. And then seeing this image,
just really Illustrated, you know, how

00:20:27.679 --> 00:20:33.110
important the photography was for
understanding these sorts of things. So,

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:38.840
our final image today is actually not an
image that was taken through research,

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:45.200
but is really an image taken of research.
So, Lina Colucci is going to share the

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:49.060
final image before we move into Q&amp;A.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:52.490
[Lina] Hi, everyone. My name is Lina Colucci. I have

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:56.179
a PhD from MIT and Harvard Medical
School. I

00:20:56.179 --> 00:21:00.139
wrapped that up a couple years ago and,
since then, I actually co-founded a

00:21:00.139 --> 00:21:04.789
boutique data science consulting company
called Edge Analytics. We're out in the

00:21:04.789 --> 00:21:08.840
Bay Area and, basically, companies bring
us in to help us solve their really

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:13.369
difficult machine learning challenges.
We work with big Fortune 500 companies,

00:21:13.369 --> 00:21:18.110
we work with some of Silicon Valley's
top startups, and we're with them from

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:23.240
early prototyping of potential solutions,
de-risking, all the way to, ultimately,

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:29.990
building and shipping the best ones.
The photo that I'm going to share with

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:48.440
you today is one that has a really
interesting backstory. So, this is... I'm

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:51.590
gonna get into exactly what you're
looking at in a second. Essentially

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:58.929
one of my lab mates at MIT asked me to
help her photograph her PhD research.

00:21:58.929 --> 00:22:06.169
She works on novel cancer treatments and
essentially she developed these

00:22:06.169 --> 00:22:11.840
materials that are filled with cancer-treating drugs in them. Essentially,

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:16.789
you implant these materials into the
body and they slowly release these

00:22:16.789 --> 00:22:23.559
cancer-treating drugs and provide the
person's chemotherapy in a long-term way.

00:22:23.559 --> 00:22:28.820
The goal, however, is that you want to
insert these implants to treat someone's

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:33.499
cancer in as a minimal type of surgery as
possible. So, you don't want to have a big

00:22:33.499 --> 00:22:38.929
surgery. So, she came up with the idea of
creating these implants and folding them

00:22:38.929 --> 00:22:44.779
like origami. So that this material
can fold up really small and through

00:22:44.779 --> 00:22:49.249
something called a laparoscopic surgery, you can insert this material through

00:22:49.249 --> 00:22:54.019
very tiny incision. But then once it's
actually inside the body, the material

00:22:54.019 --> 00:23:00.710
opens up and can spread through a larger
of the body and treat a big area of the

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:06.109
body. In this case, it would be the
abdomen. So, when she came and asked me to

00:23:06.109 --> 00:23:10.759
help her photograph these implants, it was a little bit challenging

00:23:10.759 --> 00:23:16.029
because the ink lines are white, or
transparent, so they don't necessarily

00:23:16.029 --> 00:23:21.860
photograph well. So, the interesting
backstory is, we came up with the idea

00:23:21.860 --> 00:23:27.590
actually using origami paper itself and
then playing around with lights to kind

00:23:27.590 --> 00:23:32.169
of help create these interesting shadows
and coloring effects of these implants.

00:23:32.169 --> 00:23:37.669
So, here you can see some of the behind-the-scenes photos that we took in

00:23:37.669 --> 00:23:43.879
creating this image. So, playing around
with squeezing the implants, different

00:23:43.879 --> 00:23:51.230
shapes, lighting origami paper in the
background. And what you can see is the

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:56.990
different variety and types of images
that we were able to take through these

00:23:56.990 --> 00:24:03.559
techniques. This is an interesting video
we took at the end that really kind of

00:24:03.559 --> 00:24:09.889
shows the beauty of these origami folds.
Really, the clever part of her work

00:24:09.889 --> 00:24:16.580
is using this really ancient art form of
origami to a new type of cancer

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:22.039
treatment, something that can help people
in the modern world today. So, with that, I

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:24.340
will pass it back to Erika.

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:33.100
[Erika] Thank you,
Lina. Yeah. We wanted to close with, close

00:24:33.110 --> 00:24:38.500
the sharing with that, in part, because
Lina did such a great job of explaining,

00:24:38.500 --> 00:24:43.009
kind of, the choices that she made in
taking these images. Even though this

00:24:43.009 --> 00:24:47.870
was, kind of, a macroscale device, I think
all the images that we are looking at

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:52.879
today, there were choices made in how to
