00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:44.970
[Quinton]
So, I think one important thing, just with

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:48.870
scientific imaging, is there are a lot of
different tools that you can use, but I

00:25:48.870 --> 00:25:52.230
really think the narrative behind the
stories really brings it home. So, just

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:55.050
from the other panelists, I was very
impressed about your storytelling

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:58.650
abilities and I think really having that
background on that story

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:02.490
really elevates the meaning of those
pictures. So, for me, for example, when I

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:05.790
show my friends pictures of cells,
they're like, "I don't understand what

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:09.090
this is." But when you put it, when you
contextualize it, I think that really

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:12.560
elevates the importance of the picture.

00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:15.960
[Matt] I
will add my own thoughts on it. But one

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:22.290
of my advisors, at some point, told me
that I was trying to make my images too

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:30.030
pretty. And I think, kind of, appreciating
the inherit noise, and complexity, and

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:37.740
maybe ugly parts of biology (and by
ugly, I just mean not uniform, not, you

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:42.660
know, so tight) kind of appreciating
that's there's beauty in that, as well. I

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:48.400
think that goes a long way.

00:26:48.560 --> 00:26:53.180
[Lina] I guess I'll just add some thoughts from purely, you know, a photographic

00:26:53.180 --> 00:26:58.730
perspective. I totally agree with the
narrative and what Quentin and Matt

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:02.990
have said. From purely photographic
perspective, it only helps to have a

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:06.770
really high-quality photo. Right. So, what
that means is, things in focus, really

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:12.920
nice composition, lighting, crops. Like,
that having a beautiful, you know, it doesn't

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:17.300
have to be beautiful in the "nice-looking"
way, but a nicely composed photo

00:27:17.300 --> 00:27:23.060
with good technique, is what's going to make
someone pause and be like, "Oh wow! That's

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:29.470
really interesting. What is that?" So,
good photographic technique really helps.

00:27:29.590 --> 00:27:34.610
[Erika] Excellent. Thank you. We have somebody who
would love to hear from you about how

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:39.440
the process of imaging your science has
helped shape your thinking about your

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:41.880
research.

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:47.500
[Calvin] I could take this one. So, I
guess, every time I try to think of an

00:27:47.510 --> 00:27:50.090
of,
you know, a research project or something,

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:56.990
I try to think about how my project makes sense

00:27:56.990 --> 00:28:03.680
in terms of like... or rather, like, what
is the context of the project in the

00:28:03.680 --> 00:28:07.520
broader world? So,
every time I do something, I think about

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:13.580
like, why people should care about this
and how I can present these results to

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:19.040
the world and convince people that
they're significant. I think that's

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:23.840
actually a really helpful process
because, as researchers, you know

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:28.430
we like go and read many papers, and then
we say, "oh, well there are like, you know, a

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:31.940
hundred, you know, questions that seem
interesting." But, when you like, sort of,

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:36.140
zoom out for a bit, and think about how
you're going to convey that to the world, I

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:39.920
think it's a really good exercise in
learning how to triage your priorities

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:44.120
because oftentimes we're limited, not by
like what we can do, but will we have the

00:28:44.120 --> 00:28:47.990
time and where we best want to
spend our effort doing. So, that's at least one

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:54.200
way that the process of, not just
imaging, but also communicating my ideas

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:59.680
guides where, literally, what I spend
my days doing.

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:05.299
[Sudha] Can I add a few words to what Calvin had
to say? Actually, I completely agree with

00:29:05.299 --> 00:29:13.789
him. So, my research actually largely
images things as they're happening. So,

00:29:13.789 --> 00:29:18.679
my research relies a lot on basically
evidence gathering, through looking at

00:29:18.679 --> 00:29:25.369
pictures, and basically the part and
parcel of the whole

00:29:25.369 --> 00:29:32.059
sort of imaging technique is that basis
of accuracy in what you are imaging and

00:29:32.059 --> 00:29:35.210
it actually tells you a lot about what
to do next.

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:41.539
So, I often would have like two parts
that I put into a system and then image

00:29:41.539 --> 00:29:46.009
how they are coming together to, in fact,
even know what could be the story and

00:29:46.009 --> 00:29:52.009
what to do next to even explore this
story further. So, imaging is a very

00:29:52.009 --> 00:29:58.789
powerful tool for the scientific process
and it can tell you a lot on day-to-day

00:29:58.789 --> 00:30:04.039
basis and this might just generally
involve that. Yeah. So, it can definitely

00:30:04.039 --> 00:30:13.039
change your scientific exploration to a
great extent. Yeah. So, basically it is a

00:30:13.039 --> 00:30:20.180
very powerful tool and very powerful
influencer in my research areas.

00:30:20.180 --> 00:30:24.390
[Erika] We have a question from Rachel. How much
do you think about your photos in the

00:30:24.390 --> 00:30:27.930
layout? Do you plan it out? I know Sudha,
you just said you take pictures of

00:30:27.930 --> 00:30:31.860
things as they happen, but how about
everybody? Do you plan it out or

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:34.770
do you take a bunch of image and test a
lot of things until you find an image

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:39.480
that works for you?

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:44.340
[Quinton] So, kind of just in my
approach with science, we actually try to

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:48.690
take as many pictures as we can. I'm
looking at biological phenomenon. As Matt

00:30:48.690 --> 00:30:52.320
was discussing, there's a lot of noise,
and a lot of background. So, you really

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:57.030
want to try to capture as many events as
you can possibly, really dictating that

00:30:57.030 --> 00:31:00.150
phenomenon that you're trying to
describe or observe. But then sometimes,

00:31:00.150 --> 00:31:04.470
you have very unique things that pop out
from those mosaic of images that you

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:07.770
take. But generally, in my approach, I try
to take as many different types of

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:12.280
pictures to really capture the
phenomenon that I'm studying.

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:20.640
[Matt] I think, for me, a lot of times, I'm thinking about the
magnification in which I'm taking the

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:27.180
picture. Sometimes, you know, we want to
get really close to individual cell, but

00:31:27.180 --> 00:31:31.140
then sometimes, we kind of want to take a
step back and take a larger image, which

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:37.560
might require, like, composite of many
images to be taken. So, and those, for

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:43.650
example, the image I took probably took a
couple hours to be able to acquire. So, I

00:31:43.650 --> 00:31:49.980
think a lot of times, I play around with
setting up this kind of like composite

00:31:49.980 --> 00:31:56.190
or individual and seeing what looks good.
I do have to say that all my images, I

00:31:56.190 --> 00:32:02.550
tend to think about they're going to be
shown or seen as, like, a thumb size. These

00:32:02.550 --> 00:32:09.000
are going to be small little panels in, like,
a much larger, data rich, hopefully, figure.

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:14.730
But the idea I... sometimes the higher
magnification is a little easier to show

00:32:14.730 --> 00:32:18.410
in a small inset.

00:32:21.740 --> 00:32:27.600
[Erika] Great. Anyone else? Okay, so we have a
question from Jodi here. So, obviously,

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:31.610
we're all taking these pictures cause we
love science, and we're doing science, and

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:37.260
how important is the imaging when you're
trying to get new funding or applying

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:42.560
for a new project? What role do those
images play?

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:51.440
[Matt] So, typically, when we are trying to get
new funding, we have to write grants and

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:57.450
grants typically include preliminary
data. I think having a good image

00:32:57.450 --> 00:33:05.130
won't necessarily, you know, get you those
dollar bills, but it certainly... The

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:09.390
comments I've gotten from reviewers is
that they would say, "this is really

00:33:09.390 --> 00:33:15.240
high-quality preliminary data." I
think part of that lingo is about just

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:21.210
the striking-ness of the images, but
together with a lot more quantification,

00:33:21.210 --> 00:33:24.960
and the story as a whole. It's not solely
based on the image, but I think it goes a

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:27.640
long way.

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:33.120
[Erika] Did anyone want to add to that? Great.

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:39.440
[Quinton] Just one, kind of, minor note. Sometimes, I really like the sentiment that seeing is believing.

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:45.140
Right. So, it really just drives your point home. Like, at least the type of work that I demonstrated, really

00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:49.520
seeing the nice oriented structures is a
really nice proof of concept to really

00:33:49.520 --> 00:33:53.059
drive some of your ideas home. So, if you
