00:35:16.569 --> 00:35:22.940
[Calvin] Sounds good. Thanks, Erika. So, I
really like this photo of a pot of water

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:29.329
on a stove. It actually really stood
out to me, not because the subject was

00:35:29.329 --> 00:35:34.640
particularly unusual (like, everyone boils
a pot of water every day), but I think

00:35:34.640 --> 00:35:39.170
this is really indicative of the
scientific process in physics and

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:43.130
astronomy, where a lot of the times there
are processes that you want to observe

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:47.839
and you don't have direct access to that.
So, if you want to see fire, you look at

00:35:47.839 --> 00:35:51.829
the pot of boiling water that's on top
of it. I think that's really the image

00:35:51.829 --> 00:35:56.359
here, right? You don't... like, we all,
intuitively, can make that scientific

00:35:56.359 --> 00:36:00.559
leap. You know, even without seeing the
flames, or without feeling the heat, you

00:36:00.559 --> 00:36:04.670
look at this pot of water, you look at
the way that bubbles are formed -- these

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:08.809
are very different from soap bubbles -- and
you already know that there's

00:36:08.809 --> 00:36:12.410
a phase transition between
the liquid and the gas phases of the

00:36:12.410 --> 00:36:16.700
water. That's probably caused by
convection from below and there's a heat

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:20.660
source underneath that, and that's why we
include that at

00:36:20.660 --> 00:36:26.720
the bottom of this pot there is a gas fire making these

00:36:26.720 --> 00:36:31.970
bubbles. So, you know, we prove the
existence of fire every day just by

00:36:31.970 --> 00:36:34.660
looking at this image and I think that's
pretty cool.

00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:37.220
[Erika] I love that -- the story within the story.

