00:37:11.990 --> 00:37:15.680
Like that instant, especially in a
science photograph, is that opportunity

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:21.109
to teach and learn, right? Because then
someone's going to look at the title, or

00:37:21.109 --> 00:37:25.579
caption, and learn something and this,
Calvin has expanded upon ,it's an

00:37:25.579 --> 00:37:29.790
opportunity to teach, you know, about
phase shifts or,

00:37:29.790 --> 00:37:35.280
you know, different phases of water and
boiling. Whatever the direction the

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:41.160
photographer wants to take the teaching
in, this photograph is just an invitation for that.

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:47.160
[Erika] Alright, well now
we're cooking. Sounds good. I think

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:49.380
Quinton, I think you're up next then.

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:52.080
[Quinton] So, I
might be a little biased in this picture.

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:56.640
I'm from New Mexico, so immediately the
cacti spikes, like, came out to me. But,

00:37:56.640 --> 00:38:01.050
what I really enjoy about this picture,
is just that the complexity in terms of

00:38:01.050 --> 00:38:04.980
the organization in the architecture. So,
you see a lot of order, you see the

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:09.120
spikes in a very particular manner. And
with the image across the purple

00:38:09.120 --> 00:38:12.960
backdrop, it really highlights the
different cacti. So, I

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:17.400
really enjoyed this image, not only from
me kind of being a little homesick and

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:20.820
reminding me of New Mexico, but also
being able to capture the different

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:23.840
architectures within them.

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:26.400
[Matt] I just
wanted to add that there's so many

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:32.730
beautiful images of nature. It seemed
that a lot of people are very inspired

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:38.370
by being outdoors and I think, especially
when we're experiencing a lot of, you

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:42.568
know, being locked up, but I agree this is
great.

00:38:42.568 --> 00:38:46.080
[Erika] Yes. One thing that I didn't have

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:49.350
time to talk about in the opening
webinar... and I'm going to, kind of, jump in

00:38:49.350 --> 00:38:53.240
and hijack this a little bit and talk
about my own graduate work in

00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:58.290
understanding how people make sense of
images. What I did with my graduate

00:38:58.290 --> 00:39:03.540
work, is I looked at how people make
sense of science imagery using an arts

00:39:03.540 --> 00:39:07.770
education framework. So, one of the
frameworks I used was the Tate Modern's,

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:12.900
"Ways of Looking," which says that we look
at images in a variety of ways, and

00:39:12.900 --> 00:39:16.170
artwork in a variety of ways, and the
first thing we do is find that personal

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:20.400
connection and I heard that from
Quinton there. Then after that, we

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.890
want to know, we want to look at the
subject and say, "Well, what is this a

00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:29.670
picture of? What's going on here?
What's happening?" Which is, of course, a

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:34.140
key element of science. But then, even
beyond that, people want to look at the

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:39.150
object, at the photograph, or the artwork,
or the composition as something that

00:39:39.150 --> 00:39:43.620
was deliberately created. What did you
use to take this?

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:47.340
Why did you choose these colors? What's
going on?  What are the physical

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:52.110
properties of the image itself? Then,
the fourth way of looking, is just

00:39:52.110 --> 00:39:56.460
looking at this wider context. Why did we
take this image? What was going on in the

00:39:56.460 --> 00:40:00.090
world when this photograph was taken?
I think that's something that really

00:40:00.090 --> 00:40:06.920
comes out in all of these photos.
All right. Lina, would you like to share an image with us?

00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:13.500
[Lina] Sure, let me share my
screen. All right, so, I've touched on this

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:17.100
before, but what I really love about
photographs, and especially science

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:22.230
photographs, is a photo that makes you
pause for some reason. It could be, like,

00:40:22.230 --> 00:40:27.000
that it's just a beautiful image, it
could be that you kind of do a

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:30.720
double-take, you're like, "What am I looking
at?" And then, once you have that hook, that

00:40:30.720 --> 00:40:35.220
someone's paused in the midst of their
busy day, that's an opportunity to go and

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:41.670
teach something. The reason I love
this photo... and I guess I'll say, before

00:40:41.670 --> 00:40:45.360
I dive into this one in
particular, there were so many

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:49.530
fantastic photos. Anyone who submitted a
photo should be incredibly proud. There

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:55.140
were so many really good ones.
I ultimately picked this one, a) because I

00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:58.920
thought it was so beautiful. The colors
are stunning, the composition is stunning.

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:04.680
It's this really ethereal quality of a
photo and then, so it just made me pause.

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:09.390
Again, then that's an opportunity to
go and teach something. So, I feel like

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:13.980
that once you have someone's attention
here, it's an opportunity to teach about

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:19.950
surface tension, or ripple patterns, and
wave formations, and equations for the,

00:41:19.950 --> 00:41:25.260
you know, crest and trough of waves. So,
there's a lot that, kind of, can be taught

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:28.338
once you have that person's attention.

00:41:28.338 --> 00:41:31.740
[Sudha] So,
one thing that I really like about this

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:39.150
image is that it freezes the motion,
which is a very hard thing to do

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:43.940
technically, and also it's very beautiful
to look at. So, it's like a drop that's

00:41:43.940 --> 00:41:50.760
basically is frozen in time and those
images are actually very powerful because

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:55.830
they're telling the story in time, but in
one frame. So, it's a beautifully captured

00:41:55.830 --> 00:42:01.180
phenomena. I think
that's the, sort of, very nice feature of this

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:05.040
image that I really like.

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:06.760
[Erika] Alright, Calvin ,why
don't you go ahead now.

00:42:07.320 --> 00:42:12.480
[Calvin] One neat detail I notice, is that you'll notice that around the droplet, there are these two rings of

00:42:12.490 --> 00:42:19.990
water propagating outwards. The fact
that the outer ring is much

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:24.580
more smeared out than the inner one,
tells you that the speed of surface

00:42:24.580 --> 00:42:29.800
waves on this water is different for
different wavelengths. So,

00:42:29.800 --> 00:42:33.940
that's a pretty non-trivial thing to notice just

00:42:33.940 --> 00:42:38.580
in this picture. There's a lot of very
interesting detail captured here.

00:42:40.280 --> 00:42:46.650
[Erika] Terrific. Thanks. Yes, I love the capturing
of time and space in these things.

00:42:46.650 --> 00:42:50.880
Both of those things are essential for running a science experiment or

00:42:50.880 --> 00:42:56.140
what's happening in our world. Time and
space matter. All right.

00:42:56.340 --> 00:43:01.400
But Matt, let's see your choice.

00:43:04.100 --> 00:43:11.930
[Matt] I take the field, but this one really
spoke to me because I have this motto in

00:43:11.930 --> 00:43:18.890
lab that everything is data. So, I carry
my iPhone with me everywhere and iPhone

00:43:18.890 --> 00:43:26.500
cameras are so good nowadays that I, kind
of, do this a lot where I put my camera

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:32.330
onto the ocular of the microscope. So,
you end up getting this kind of ring,

00:43:32.330 --> 00:43:38.630
which is the ocular, but then you get the
image through. I typically will crop

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:44.300
it, you know, and just use the image
inside. But, I love the fact that this

00:43:44.300 --> 00:43:51.440
tells a very clear message, at least to
me as a scientist, of where that

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:56.750
photographer was. I feel like I
understand the process. I'm seeing it

00:43:56.750 --> 00:44:01.880
as a person saw it. You know, there
were a series of images

00:44:01.880 --> 00:44:08.630
posted with the same kind of photograph
from a microscope with just different

00:44:08.630 --> 00:44:19.250
maybe substrates being imaged. But, I
think that when you can capture the

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:24.020
process in the image, I think that could
be really powerful too. So, this is kind

00:44:24.020 --> 00:44:34.240
of what spoke to me about the series of
phone to the ocular images.

00:44:34.240 --> 00:44:41.900
[Sudha] I have a comment on that. It's a great choice, Matt. I also like the part that the texture of

00:44:41.900 --> 00:44:47.650
the subject is so nicely visible in the
image, which is not usually the case.

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:53.060
Normally, there's a compromise between contrast
and texture and here that doesn't seem to

00:44:53.060 --> 00:45:00.340
be the case. So, it's really commendable
effort on technicality and it's a really great image.

00:45:02.420 --> 00:45:05.140
[Erika] Yeah, I think that
comparison can be really powerful in an

00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:09.680
image. One of the things we talked about
with Keith and Felice, is making the

00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:13.880
decision of what to photograph, and what
not to photograph. And what you show is

00:45:13.880 --> 00:45:19.280
often dependent on what you don't show,
or the choices you make of how you

00:45:19.280 --> 00:45:29.060
compare things. Absolutely. Alright,
Sudha, is your is your image ready to go? There it is.

00:45:29.060 --> 00:45:35.600
[Sudha] So, apologies for the
interruption. I like this image

00:45:35.600 --> 00:45:41.270
because, like Lina was saying, that an
interesting aspect to the image is that it

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:47.000
may... if it will incite you to stop and
pause for a moment and think a little

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:52.280
bit about the image. Maybe the process of
how it was taken, the story around it, and

00:45:52.280 --> 00:45:57.950
also a message that is coming through.
I felt that this image was combining

00:45:57.950 --> 00:46:02.330
a lot of different things and these
different things are nicely. So, first of

00:46:02.330 --> 00:46:05.930
all, the composition is really nice. The
objects, the rice grains, are nicely placed.

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:10.700
Even though they aren't
brilliant in color, the colors are very

00:46:10.700 --> 00:46:17.900
nice and overall composition is very,
very nice. What I also liked about is also

00:46:17.900 --> 00:46:24.860
my personal bias as a microscopy person.
So, I like to see small things seen in

00:46:24.860 --> 00:46:30.140
better details to see things that we
can't generally see with the naked eye,

00:46:30.140 --> 00:46:36.410
or ordinarily. So, to see beyond what our
eye can see. So, for example, if I

00:46:36.410 --> 00:46:41.900
hold a rice grain, I wouldn't know that it's
actually asymmetrical. So, then one side

00:46:41.900 --> 00:46:47.660
is more shaded than the other side.
So, there's asymmetry I didn't know

00:46:47.660 --> 00:46:53.630
existed. Then, what is this dark color?
Is it where the embryo used to sit or

00:46:53.630 --> 00:46:57.650
the fertile part of the rice grain?
Which, again, I didn't know existed

00:46:57.650 --> 00:47:03.710
in a processed rice like this. So, it's
like a collection of scientific

00:47:03.710 --> 00:47:08.480
messages that are encompassed in this
image. So, that part I really liked. I

00:47:08.480 --> 00:47:12.620
also liked the technical mystery around it.
So, I really want to know how it was

00:47:12.620 --> 00:47:17.480
taken. There seems to be some back
illumination happening,

00:47:17.480 --> 00:47:25.690
but I'm not sure. So, it seems like the
person who took the image had a good sense of

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:30.950
technicality of how to illuminate the grains to get the most information out.

00:47:30.950 --> 00:47:35.270
So, I really like that part. Yeah, so
basically, it's a combination of the

00:47:35.270 --> 00:47:41.390
composition, the visualization beyond
what we ordinarily see, and also the

00:47:41.390 --> 00:47:47.080
trick that has been used here. That's why
I really like this image.

00:47:48.070 --> 00:47:53.860
[Erika] Thank you. Would any of the other
panelists like to comment on this image?

00:47:53.860 --> 00:48:00.590
[Calvin] Yeah, I was going to ask what the little dark
flecks are. There are these big, dark

00:48:00.590 --> 00:48:04.460
spots, as you said. I think those are the embryos
of the rice grain. But, there are also

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:07.790
sort of these dark flecks and I don't.. do
you have any idea what those are or are

00:48:07.790 --> 00:48:14.270
those just, like, imperfections?
I know nothing about plants so...

00:48:14.270 --> 00:48:18.340
[Sudha] Same here. I don't have here what I can see the...
it's an interesting question. Maybe the

00:48:18.340 --> 00:48:24.410
participant could comment on it later or
any of you know about it. I don't know. It

00:48:24.410 --> 00:48:30.260
adds nice features to the grains, but
I'm not sure.

00:48:30.340 --> 00:48:36.440
[Erika] If I'm not mistaken, this image was posted on social media, which means it's there on the hashtag and

00:48:36.440 --> 00:48:42.980
perhaps that's a nice conversation to
begin online. I love this idea of... we

00:48:42.980 --> 00:48:47.390
didn't really talk about this, but it was
so important -- that a good image provokes

00:48:47.390 --> 00:48:51.710
questions. In some cases, we've seen
those, or questions about the details of

00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:56.090
what's being photographed, about the
wider context and phenomenon that's

00:48:56.090 --> 00:49:01.610
behind it. Looking for things we can't
see. You know, like, clearly you all

00:49:01.610 --> 00:49:06.170
are scientists because you've got
questions about these and the photos

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:12.020
both answer them and ask them, as well,
and I think that's really powerful.

00:49:12.060 --> 00:49:18.680
[Matt] I was just going to jump in to say that one of
our attendees, Dora Bever, is telling us

00:49:18.680 --> 00:49:22.970
these are the germs, of the germ of
the rice, and this is the part that

00:49:22.970 --> 00:49:31.940
